    },
    "q-346118": {
      "dom_id": "step-q346118",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=(x-4)^{3}(2x+1)^{2}$ and $f(0)=-5.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ to estimate the value of $f(1).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$f(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0) \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(0)=-5,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=(x-4)^{3}(2x+1)^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(0)=(0-4)^{3}(2(0)+1)^{2}=-64.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=-5-64x.\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(1)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & ≈L(1) \\\\  & =-5-64(1) \\\\  & =-69.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
