    "q-4813": {
      "dom_id": "step-q4813",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=2x$ and $f(2)=4.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ to estimate the value of $f(3).$",
      "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(2)=4,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=2x,$ then ${f}^{′}(2)=2(2)=4.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=4+4(x-2).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(3)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & ≈L(3) \\\\  & =4+4(3-2) \\\\  & =4+4(1) \\\\  & =4+4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
