    "q-4802": {
      "dom_id": "step-q4802",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=5x-3$ and $f(2)=-1.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ to estimate the value of $f(2.4).$",
      "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(2)+{f}^{′}(2)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(2)=-1,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=5x-3,$ then ${f}^{′}(2)=5(2)-3=7.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=-1+7(x-2).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(2.4)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=2.4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2.4) & ≈L(2.4) \\\\  & =-1+7(2.4-2) \\\\  & =-1+7(0.4) \\\\  & =-1+2.8 \\\\  & =1.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
