    "q-346114": {
      "dom_id": "step-q346114",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ to estimate the value of $f(\\frac{5\\pi }{18}).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$f(\\frac{5\\pi }{18})≈$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1,$ and since ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(\\frac{5\\pi }{18})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{18}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{5\\pi }{18}) & ≈L(\\frac{5\\pi }{18}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\frac{5\\pi }{18}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{36} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{72}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
