    "q-48400": {
      "dom_id": "step-q48400",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(1,4)$ and is concave down on the interval $(0,5).$ If ${f}^{′}(1)=-2,$ estimate the value of $f(0.8)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.",
      "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{′}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function passes through the point $(1,4),$ so we have $f(1)=4.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(1)=-2,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =4+(-2)(x-1) \\\\  & =4-2x+2 \\\\  & =-2x+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(0.8)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=0.8.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.8) & ≈L(0.8) \\\\  & =-2(0.8)+6 \\\\  & =-1.6+6 \\\\  & =4.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(0,5),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=1,$ and therefore we have an overestimate.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
