    "q-48404": {
      "dom_id": "step-q48404",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(3,-3)$ and is concave down on the interval $(1,4).$ If ${f}^{′}(3)=4,$ estimate the value of $f(3.2)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=3$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.",
      "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=3$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(3)+{f}^{′}(3)(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(3,-3),$ so we have $f(3)=-3.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(3)=4,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =-3+4(x-3) \\\\  & =-3+4x-12 \\\\  & =4x-15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(3.2)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=3.2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.2) & ≈L(3.2) \\\\  & =4(3.2)-15 \\\\  & =12.8-15 \\\\  & =-2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(1,4),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=3,$ and therefore we have an overestimate.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
