    "q-115342": {
      "dom_id": "step-q115342",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(-4,2)$ and is concave up on the interval $(-6,-3).$ If ${f}^{′}(-4)=-2,$ estimate the value of $f(-3.8)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-4$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.",
      "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=-4$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-4)+{f}^{′}(-4)(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(-4,2),$ so we have $f(-4)=2.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(-4)=-2,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+(-2)(x+4) \\\\  & =2-2(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-3.8)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-3.8.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3.8) & ≈L(-3.8) \\\\  & =2-2(-3.8+4) \\\\  & =2-2(0.2) \\\\  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave up on the interval $(-6,-3),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is below the graph near $x=-4,$ and therefore we have an underestimate.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
