    "q-48402": {
      "dom_id": "step-q48402",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(-2,2)$ and is concave up on the interval $(-3,-1).$ If ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1,$ estimate the value of $f(-1.8)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=-2$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.",
      "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=-2$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x-(-2)) \\\\  & =f(-2)+{f}^{′}(-2)(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function passes through the point $(-2,2),$ so we have $f(-2)=2.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(-2)=-1,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+(-1)(x+2) \\\\  & =2-(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-1.8)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=-1.8.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1.8) & ≈L(-1.8) \\\\  & =2-(-1.8+2) \\\\  & =2-0.2 \\\\  & =1.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave up on the interval $(-3,-1),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is below the graph close to $x=-2,$ and therefore we have an underestimate.",
